## Method
All steps run in one Python script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, in the `gars-bio` conda
environment (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib — all present in its lockfile), submitted
as a single batch job. Inputs are opened read-only; nothing upstream is written.

1. **Resolve inputs.** At run time, resolve `counts_gene` and `design` with
   `_system/resolve_artifact.py --project <project> --assay rnaseq_bulk --type <type>`; fail if
   either does not resolve.
2. **Load and align.** Read the count matrix (tab-separated; `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one
   column per sample). Drop `gene_name`, index by `gene_id`. Read the design CSV. Check that the
   matrix's sample columns and the design's `sample_id` values are the same set; stop with an
   error naming the mismatch if not. Order matrix columns to match the design.
3. **Filter.** Drop genes with zero counts in every sample.
4. **Normalise and transform.** Library-size normalise to counts per million (CPM, using each
   sample's column total of the length-scaled counts), then transform with `log2(CPM + 1)`.
5. **Select genes.** Keep the 500 genes with the highest variance of log2(CPM + 1) across
   samples (all remaining genes if fewer than 500 — the matrix currently has 200). Record the
   number used.
6. **PCA.** `sklearn.decomposition.PCA(n_components=min(n_samples, n_genes))` on the
   samples × genes matrix, genes mean-centred and **not** scaled to unit variance
   (`svd_solver="full"`, deterministic). Record each component's explained-variance ratio.
7. **Plot.** Scatter of PC1 (x) vs PC2 (y), one point per sample, coloured by `condition`
   with a legend, each point labelled with its `sample_id`; axis labels give the percentage of
   variance explained (e.g. `PC1 (xx.x%)`). Title: `sample-pca: log2(CPM+1), top N variable genes`.
   Save as PNG (300 dpi) and PDF.
8. **Write tables.** Sample scores (`sample_id`, `condition`, PC1…PCk) and variance explained
   (`component`, `explained_variance_ratio`, `cumulative`) as CSV.

